# A service of the system installed, started, stopped, asked and removed by
# another program: a systemd unit, `--user` or the system's, on Linux; a
# launchd agent of the user's or a daemon of the system's on macOS; a service
# of the Service Control Manager on Windows. What Go's `svc/mgr`, Rust's
# `windows-service` `ServiceManager` and the `systemctl` and `launchctl` a
# person types give, with one definition, `Definition`, for the three.
#
# What it does NOT do: be the service (`service`, the program's own side: its
# readiness, its controls, the sockets handed to it); run a service as another
# account, which the system's own tools set (`systemctl edit`, launchd's
# `UserName`, `sc config obj=`); change an installed definition in place,
# which is `remove` and `install` again; pause a service, which Windows
# alone has.
#
# **How it reaches each system.** systemd and launchd through their own
# programs, `systemctl` and `/bin/launchctl`, run with a list of words, never
# a shell, and their exit codes read; the unit file and the property list are
# written by this package, every text escaped as its format needs, so a path
# with a space, a quote or a line end reaches the program whole. The Service
# Control Manager through its own calls, in the platform layer's `hero_scm.h`.
# **This package removes only what it can tell it installed**: on systemd
# and launchd, a unit file or a property list at the path `path_of` names;
# the Service Control Manager keeps no mark of who installed a service, so
# there `remove` deletes the service of that name, whoever installed it.
#
# **Waiting.** `start`, `stop`, `restart` and `remove` wait, at most the span
# they are given, until the system says the service reached the state asked;
# a span of 0 asks and answers at once, `timeout` where the state is not
# reached yet, and the request stands either way.
#
# It fails with `invalid`, `exists`, `not_found`, `permission`, `timeout`,
# `limit`, `unsupported` and `failed` (README § A4).
#
# Not written yet, each a `pending` block of its ledger: the services a
# manager holds, listed.

use fs
use os
use plist
use service/manage/hero_scm
use service/manage/internal/checks
use service/manage/internal/failure
use service/manage/internal/job
use service/manage/internal/launchd
use service/manage/internal/scm
use service/manage/internal/systemd
use service/manage/internal/unit
use strings
use time

## Codes

# An argument out of its range: a name that is not 1 to `NAME_MOST` bytes of
# ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`; a text holding a zero byte, or a
# description or a setting systemd reads bare that it would change; a program
# or a folder that is not absolute; a variable's name that is no name; a
# restart delay out of range. Every function but `manager` fails with it.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# A service of that name is installed or known to the manager already.
# `install` fails with it.
constant EXISTS: str
    "exists"

# No service of that name is installed: no unit or job the manager knows, no
# file at `path_of`'s path for `remove`. Every function that names an
# installed service fails with it.
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    "not_found"

# The system refuses the program what the call does: a system service is
# installed, started and removed by an administrator (root on Linux and macOS).
constant PERMISSION: str
    "permission"

# The service did not reach the state asked in the span given, or a manager's
# program did not answer in a minute. `start`, `stop`, `restart` and `remove`
# fail with it.
constant TIMEOUT: str
    "timeout"

# The system has no memory left for the call.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

# This system has no manager this package reaches, or the manager has no form
# for what is asked: a Linux not booted with systemd, a user's service or a
# working folder on Windows, a restart at every exit on Windows.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

# The system refused for a reason none of the above names; the message carries
# its own words.
constant FAILED: str
    "failed"

# The longest service name, in bytes: systemd's longest unit name, 255 bytes
# with `.service` (measured with systemd 257 on 2026-11-24).
constant NAME_MOST: i64
    247

# The longest description, in bytes.
constant TEXT_MOST: i64
    32768

## What a service is

# Whose a service is.
variant Scope
    # The machine's: a systemd system unit, a launchd daemon, a service of the
    # Service Control Manager. Installed, started and removed by an
    # administrator.
    system
    # The user's who runs the program: a systemd user unit, started by the
    # user's manager; a launchd agent of the user's login session. Windows has
    # none.
    user

# When the system starts a service again after it ended by itself.
variant Restart
    # Never: an ended service stays ended until it is started.
    never
    # When it failed, as each system counts a failure: systemd's
    # `Restart=on-failure`, launchd's exit with a status other than 0 or a
    # crash, the Service Control Manager's failure actions, which also count
    # a stop the service reports with a code other than 0.
    on_failure
    # At every end, a success too: systemd's `Restart=always`, launchd's
    # `KeepAlive`. The Service Control Manager has none.
    always

# A service's definition: what `install` writes. `definition` makes one with
# the plain choices; its fields are changed as any record's are.
record Definition
    # The service's name: a systemd unit's without `.service`, a launchd
    # label, the Service Control Manager's name; 1 to `NAME_MOST` bytes of
    # ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`, the first a letter or a digit,
    # a reversed domain such as `org.example.web` by launchd's custom.
    name: str
    # What the service is, one line, "" for none: systemd's `Description=`,
    # the Service Control Manager's description; launchd's property list has
    # no key for one, so it is not written there.
    description: str
    # The program's absolute path.
    program: str
    # The program's arguments after its name, each reaching it whole.
    arguments: [str]
    # The folder the program starts in, absolute, "" for the manager's own
    # (`/` for systemd's system units and for launchd). Windows has no such
    # setting.
    dir: str
    # Variables set for the program, name to value. Windows has no such
    # setting.
    env: {str: str}
    # Whose the service is.
    scope: Scope
    # Whether the system starts it by itself: at boot for a system service,
    # at the user's login for a user's (a systemd user unit at the start of
    # the user's manager).
    at_boot: bool
    # When the system starts it again after it ended.
    restart: Restart
    # How long the system waits before it starts it again: systemd's
    # `RestartSec=` and the Service Control Manager's delay, to the
    # millisecond; on launchd the shortest time between two starts,
    # `ThrottleInterval`, in whole seconds rounded up, at least one. 0 to
    # 4,294,967,295 milliseconds; read only where `restart` restarts.
    restart_delay: time.Duration

# The manager a system has.
variant Manager
    # systemd, on a Linux booted with it.
    systemd
    # launchd, on macOS.
    launchd
    # The Service Control Manager, on Windows.
    windows

# Where a service stands, as `status_of` reads it.
variant State
    # Not running, as asked or never started.
    stopped
    # Starting: systemd's `activating`, the Service Control Manager's start
    # pending.
    starting
    # Running.
    running
    # Stopping: systemd's `deactivating`, the Service Control Manager's stop
    # pending.
    stopping
    # Asked to pause and not yet paused (Windows alone).
    pausing
    # Paused (Windows alone).
    paused
    # Asked to go on after a pause and not yet going (Windows alone).
    resuming
    # Not running, and the system holds that it failed: systemd's `failed`, a
    # launchd job whose last end had a status other than 0, a service of the
    # Service Control Manager stopped with an error code.
    failed
        # The code it ended with; on POSIX minus the signal's number where a
        # signal ended it; 0 where it failed otherwise (a start that timed
        # out, a program that could not be run).
        code: i64

# What `status_of` answers.
record Status
    # Where the service stands.
    state: State
    # Its main process's id; fails `not_found` where none runs.
    pid: i64?
    # Whether the system starts it by itself, as `Definition.at_boot` says.
    at_boot: bool

# A definition of the service `name`, of `scope`, running `program` with
# `arguments`: no description, the manager's own folder, no variables, not
# started by itself, never restarted, a restart delay of a second.
#
# - `name`: the service's name, as `Definition.name` takes it.
# - `program`: the program's absolute path.
# - `arguments`: its arguments after its name; empty for none.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
#
# Nothing is checked here: `install` checks every field.
function definition(name: str, program: str, arguments: [str], scope: Scope) -> Definition
    return Definition(
        name: name,
        description: "",
        program: program,
        arguments: arguments,
        dir: "",
        env: {},
        scope: scope,
        at_boot: false,
        restart: .never,
        restart_delay: time.seconds(1)
    )

## The system's manager

# The manager of the system this program runs on: systemd where Linux was
# booted with it (`/run/systemd/system` is a folder, `sd_booted(3)`'s test),
# launchd on macOS, the Service Control Manager on Windows.
#
# Fails `unsupported` on a Linux booted with another init (OpenRC, runit,
# a container's own first process) and on any other system.
function manager() -> Manager?
    match os.system()
        .windows => return ok(.windows)
        .darwin  => return ok(.launchd)
        .linux =>
            match fs.is_dir(systemd.BOOTED)
                .ok booted =>
                    if booted
                        return ok(.systemd)
                    return fail(
                        code: UNSUPPORTED,
                        msg: f"this Linux was not booted with systemd: no folder {systemd.BOOTED}, and no other init is reached by this package"
                    )
                .err e => return fail(code: UNSUPPORTED, msg: f"asking whether systemd booted this Linux: {e.msg}")
        .other => return fail(code: UNSUPPORTED, msg: "this system is neither Linux with systemd, macOS nor Windows")

## Definitions written

# The systemd unit file of `d`: `[Unit]` with its description, `[Service]`
# with `Type=exec`, the program and its arguments quoted, the folder, the
# variables in the order of their names, the restart and its delay, and
# `[Install]` with the target `install` enables it for.
#
# - `d`: the definition.
#
# Every word of `ExecStart=` is quoted with its `%` and `$` doubled, so the
# program receives it as it stands; a variable's value keeps its `$`, which
# `Environment=` does not expand. Fails `invalid` where a field is out of its
# range, or the description or the folder has a blank at either end or ends
# with `\`, which systemd would read otherwise. See also `format_job`,
# `install`.
function format_unit(d: Definition) -> str?
    checks.definition_checked(d, rules: .posix)?
    checks.bare_setting_checked(text: d.description, what: "the description")?
    checks.bare_setting_checked(text: d.dir, what: "the working folder")?
    return ok(unit.file_text(d, delay_ms: checks.delay_ms(d.restart_delay)?))

# The launchd property list of `d`, in XML as `plutil -convert xml1` writes
# one: `Label`, `ProgramArguments`, `WorkingDirectory`,
# `EnvironmentVariables`, `RunAtLoad`, `KeepAlive` and `ThrottleInterval`.
#
# - `d`: the definition.
#
# A restart on failure keeps the job waiting to be started where it is not
# started at boot (`AfterInitialDemand`). Fails `invalid` where a field is out
# of its range or a text holds a character XML cannot hold; `unsupported`
# where `d` restarts at every exit and is not started at boot, which launchd
# cannot hold. See also `format_unit`, `install`.
function format_job(d: Definition) -> str?
    checks.definition_checked(d, rules: .posix)?
    v = job.property_list(d, delay_ms: checks.delay_ms(d.restart_delay)?)?
    return plist.format_xml(v)

# Where `install` writes the definition of the service `name` of `scope` on
# this system: `/etc/systemd/system/<name>.service` or the user's
# `systemd/user` folder under `os.config_dir`; `/Library/LaunchDaemons` or the
# user's `Library/LaunchAgents`, `<name>.plist`.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
#
# Nothing is asked of the disk. Fails `invalid` for a name out of its range;
# `unsupported` on Windows, whose Service Control Manager keeps its services
# in no file a program writes, and where `manager` does; and as `os.home_dir`
# does for a user's.
function path_of(name: str, scope: Scope) -> str?
    checks.name_checked(name)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.path_of(name, scope: scope)
        .launchd => return launchd.path_of(name, scope: scope)
        .windows =>
            return fail(
                code: UNSUPPORTED,
                msg: f"the Service Control Manager keeps the service \"{name}\" in the system's own database, in no file a program writes"
            )

## Installing and removing

# Installs `d`, which the system then starts as `d` says, or when asked; the
# service does not start now.
#
# - `d`: the definition.
#
# On systemd: the unit file written at `path_of`'s path, `systemctl
# daemon-reload`, and `systemctl enable` where `d.at_boot`. On launchd: the
# property list written there, which launchd loads at the next boot or login,
# or at `start`. On Windows: `CreateServiceW`, the program's path and
# arguments quoted as the C runtime reads them back, run as the machine's own
# account, with its description and failure actions; a step the system
# refuses undoes the steps before it.
#
# Fails `invalid` where a field is out of its range; `exists` where a unit, a
# job or a service of that name is installed or loaded already; `permission`
# where the system refuses (a system service asks an administrator);
# `unsupported` where `manager` does, for a user's service, a working folder,
# a variable or a restart at every exit on Windows, and for a restart at every
# exit not started at boot on launchd; `failed` with the system's words
# otherwise. See also `remove`, `format_unit`, `format_job`.
function install(d: Definition) -> ()?
    match manager()?
        .systemd =>
            text = format_unit(d)?
            return systemd.install(d, text: text)
        .launchd =>
            text = format_job(d)?
            return launchd.install(d, text: text)
        .windows =>
            checks.definition_checked(d, rules: .windows)?
            return scm.install(d, delay_ms: checks.delay_ms(d.restart_delay)?)

# Stops the service `name` of `scope`, waiting at most `limit`, and removes it.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
# - `limit`: how long the stop may take; 0 asks and waits not at all.
#
# On systemd: stopped, `systemctl disable`, the unit file deleted, `systemctl
# daemon-reload`, and its failed state reset. On launchd: `launchctl bootout`
# where it is loaded, its process waited for, the property list deleted. On
# Windows: stopped and `DeleteService`, which the system ends once no program
# holds the service.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no unit file or property list stands at `path_of`'s
# path, or no service of the name on Windows; `timeout` where the stop takes
# longer than `limit`, the definition then left in place; and as `stop` does.
# See also `install`.
function remove(name: str, scope: Scope, limit: time.Duration) -> ()?
    checks.name_checked(name)?
    checks.limit_checked(limit)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.remove(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .launchd => return launchd.remove(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .windows => return scm.remove(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)

## Starting and stopping

# Starts the service `name` of `scope`, waiting at most `limit` until the
# system says it started; a service that runs already is left running.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
# - `limit`: how long the start may take; 0 asks and answers at once.
#
# On systemd `systemctl start`, done once the program runs; on launchd
# `launchctl bootstrap` where the job is not loaded, then `launchctl
# kickstart`; on Windows `StartServiceW`, done once the service leaves its
# start pending.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no such service is installed; `timeout` where it is
# not started in `limit`; `failed` where the system says the start failed,
# with its words; `permission` and `unsupported` as `install` does. See also
# `stop`, `restart`, `status_of`.
function start(name: str, scope: Scope, limit: time.Duration) -> ()?
    checks.name_checked(name)?
    checks.limit_checked(limit)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.start(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .launchd => return launchd.start(name, scope: scope)
        .windows => return scm.start(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)

# Stops the service `name` of `scope`, waiting at most `limit` until its
# process has ended; a stopped service is left stopped. The system does not
# restart a service it was asked to stop.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
# - `limit`: how long the stop may take; 0 asks and answers at once.
#
# On systemd `systemctl stop`; on launchd `launchctl bootout`, which also
# unloads the job until the next `start`, boot or login, since a job kept
# alive would be started again; on Windows the stop control, done once the
# service reports stopped.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no such service is installed; `timeout` where it has
# not stopped in `limit`, the system still stopping it; `permission` and
# `unsupported` as `install` does. See also `start`, `restart`.
function stop(name: str, scope: Scope, limit: time.Duration) -> ()?
    checks.name_checked(name)?
    checks.limit_checked(limit)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.stop(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .launchd => return launchd.stop(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .windows => return scm.stop(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)

# Stops the service `name` of `scope` where it runs and starts it, waiting at
# most `limit` for both; a stopped one is started.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
# - `limit`: how long both may take; 0 asks and answers at once.
#
# On systemd `systemctl restart`; on launchd `launchctl kickstart -k` where
# the job is loaded, else as `start`; on Windows `stop` then `start`.
#
# Fails as `stop` and `start` do. See also `start`, `stop`.
function restart(name: str, scope: Scope, limit: time.Duration) -> ()?
    checks.name_checked(name)?
    checks.limit_checked(limit)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.restart(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)
        .launchd => return launchd.restart(name, scope: scope)
        .windows => return scm.restart(name, scope: scope, limit: limit)

## Asking

# Where the service `name` of `scope` stands, its main process and whether
# the system starts it by itself.
#
# - `name`: the service's name.
# - `scope`: whose the service is.
#
# On systemd `systemctl show`'s `ActiveState`, `MainPID` and
# `UnitFileState`; on launchd `launchctl list`'s columns where the job is
# loaded, `stopped` where only its property list stands, and the list's
# `RunAtLoad` or `KeepAlive`; on Windows `QueryServiceStatusEx` and
# `QueryServiceConfigW`.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no such service is installed or loaded;
# `permission` on macOS for a system daemon asked by a program that is not
# root, since `launchctl list` lists the system's jobs to root alone and the
# only other answer, `launchctl print`, says its output is no interface;
# `unsupported` as `install` does; `failed` where systemd answers a state
# this package does not know. See also `start`, `stop`.
function status_of(name: str, scope: Scope) -> Status?
    checks.name_checked(name)?

    match manager()?
        .systemd => return systemd.status_of(name, scope: scope)
        .launchd => return launchd.status_of(name, scope: scope)
        .windows => return scm.status_of(name, scope: scope)

## Tests

test "definition: the plain choices, and a field changed as any record's"
    d @= definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "/usr/bin/web", arguments: ["--port", "8080"], scope: .user)
    assert d.description == ""
    assert d.dir == ""
    assert d.env.len() == 0
    assert d.at_boot == false
    assert d.restart == .never
    assert d.restart_delay.nanoseconds == 1000000000
    d.at_boot @ true
    assert d.at_boot

test "format_unit: every setting, a program with a space, arguments that a shell or systemd would read otherwise"
    d @= definition(
        name: "org.example.web",
        program: "/opt/my web/web",
        arguments: ["--motd", "100% \"up\" $HOME", ""],
        scope: .system
    )
    d.description @ "A web server, 100% up"
    d.dir @ "/srv/my web"
    d.env @ {"PORT": "8080", "GREETING": "a $b\nc"}
    d.at_boot @ true
    d.restart @ .on_failure
    d.restart_delay @ time.milliseconds(1500)
    want = [
        "[Unit]"
        "Description=A web server, 100%% up"
        ""
        "[Service]"
        "Type=exec"
        "ExecStart=\"/opt/my web/web\" \"--motd\" \"100%% \\\"up\\\" $$HOME\" \"\""
        "WorkingDirectory=/srv/my web"
        "Environment=\"GREETING=a $b\\nc\""
        "Environment=\"PORT=8080\""
        "Restart=on-failure"
        "RestartSec=1500ms"
        ""
        "[Install]"
        "WantedBy=multi-user.target"
        ""
    ]
    assert format_unit(d).must() == join(want, "\n")

test "format_unit: a user's unit with no description, never restarted, wanted by the user's default target"
    d = definition(name: "a", program: "/bin/true", arguments: [], scope: .user)
    want = "[Unit]\n\n[Service]\nType=exec\nExecStart=\"/bin/true\"\nRestart=no\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=default.target\n"
    assert format_unit(d).must() == want

test "format_unit: refusals, a relative program, a description of two lines or with a blank at an end, a folder ending in a backslash, a zero byte"
    d @= definition(name: "a", program: "bin/true", arguments: [], scope: .user)
    assert format_unit(d).is_err()
    d.program @ "/bin/true"
    d.description @ "two\nlines"
    assert format_unit(d).is_err()
    d.description @ "blank "
    assert format_unit(d).is_err()
    d.description @ ""
    d.dir @ "/srv/x\\"
    assert format_unit(d).is_err()
    d.dir @ "srv"
    assert format_unit(d).is_err()
    d.dir @ ""
    zero: [u8] = [97, 0]
    d.arguments @ [zero.validated_bytes().must()]

    match format_unit(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

test "format_job: every key, the arguments as strings XML escapes, a restart on failure waiting to be started"
    d @= definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "/opt/my web/web", arguments: ["<a & b>", "\"q\""], scope: .user)
    d.description @ "not written"
    d.dir @ "/srv"
    d.env @ {"PORT": "8080"}
    d.restart @ .on_failure
    d.restart_delay @ time.milliseconds(1500)
    want = [
        "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>"
        "<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\" \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">"
        "<plist version=\"1.0\">"
        "<dict>"
        "\t<key>Label</key>"
        "\t<string>org.example.web</string>"
        "\t<key>ProgramArguments</key>"
        "\t<array>"
        "\t\t<string>/opt/my web/web</string>"
        "\t\t<string>&lt;a &amp; b&gt;</string>"
        "\t\t<string>\"q\"</string>"
        "\t</array>"
        "\t<key>WorkingDirectory</key>"
        "\t<string>/srv</string>"
        "\t<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>"
        "\t<dict>"
        "\t\t<key>PORT</key>"
        "\t\t<string>8080</string>"
        "\t</dict>"
        "\t<key>RunAtLoad</key>"
        "\t<false/>"
        "\t<key>KeepAlive</key>"
        "\t<dict>"
        "\t\t<key>SuccessfulExit</key>"
        "\t\t<false/>"
        "\t\t<key>Crashed</key>"
        "\t\t<true/>"
        "\t\t<key>AfterInitialDemand</key>"
        "\t\t<true/>"
        "\t</dict>"
        "\t<key>ThrottleInterval</key>"
        "\t<integer>2</integer>"
        "</dict>"
        "</plist>"
        ""
    ]
    assert format_job(d).must() == join(want, "\n")

test "format_job: a restart at every exit is kept alive at boot, and refused where the job waits to be started"
    d @= definition(name: "a", program: "/bin/true", arguments: [], scope: .user)
    d.restart @ .always

    match format_job(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED

    d.at_boot @ true
    assert strings.contains(s: format_job(d).must(), substr: "<key>KeepAlive</key>\n\t<true/>")

test "format_job: a control character no XML holds is refused"
    d = definition(name: "a", program: "/bin/true", arguments: ["bell\u{7}"], scope: .user)

    match format_job(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

test "manager: launchd on macOS, the Service Control Manager on Windows, systemd or a refusal on Linux"
    match os.system()
        .darwin  => assert manager().must() == .launchd
        .windows => assert manager().must() == .windows
        .linux =>
            booted = fs.is_dir(systemd.BOOTED).must()

            match manager()
                .ok m  => assert booted && m == .systemd
                .err e => assert !booted && e.code == UNSUPPORTED
        .other => assert manager().is_err()

test "path_of: the user's agents on macOS, the system's units on a systemd Linux, none on Windows"
    match manager()
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")
        .ok m =>
            match m
                .launchd =>
                    home = os.home_dir().must()
                    assert path_of("org.example.web", scope: .user).must() == home + "/Library/LaunchAgents/org.example.web.plist"
                    assert path_of("org.example.web", scope: .system).must() == "/Library/LaunchDaemons/org.example.web.plist"
                .systemd => assert path_of("web", scope: .system).must() == "/etc/systemd/system/web.service"
                .windows => assert path_of("web", scope: .system).is_err()

    assert path_of("a/b", scope: .user).is_err()

test "status_of: a service nobody installed is not found, and a name out of range is invalid"
    match manager()
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")
        .ok _ =>
            match status_of("org.heroes-lang.surely-not-installed-5150", scope: .user)
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == UNSUPPORTED

    assert status_of("", scope: .user).is_err()

test "remove and stop: a service nobody installed is not found, a limit below zero is invalid"
    match manager()
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")
        .ok m =>
            scope: Scope = if m == .windows
                .system
            else
                .user

            match remove("org.heroes-lang.surely-not-installed-5150", scope: scope, limit: time.nanoseconds(0))
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

    assert stop("a", scope: .user, limit: time.nanoseconds(-1)).is_err()

test "install on Windows: a user's service, a working folder and a restart at every exit refused by words"
    if hero_scm.supported() == 0
        skip_test(reason: "only Windows has the Service Control Manager")
    d @= definition(
        name: "org.heroes-lang.never-made",
        program: "C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe",
        arguments: [],
        scope: .user
    )

    match install(d)
        .ok _ => assert false
        .err e =>
            assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED
            assert e.msg == failure.NO_USER_SERVICES

    d.scope @ .system
    d.dir @ "C:\\"

    match install(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED

    d.dir @ ""
    d.restart @ .always

    match install(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED

## Examples

# The unit file a systemd user unit is written as.
test "format_unit: a user's web server, restarted on failure"
    d @= definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "/usr/local/bin/web", arguments: ["--port", "8080"], scope: .user)
    d.restart @ .on_failure
    text = format_unit(d).must()
    assert strings.contains(s: text, substr: "ExecStart=\"/usr/local/bin/web\" \"--port\" \"8080\"")
    assert strings.contains(s: text, substr: "Restart=on-failure")

# The property list a launchd agent is written as.
test "format_job: a user's agent started at login"
    d @= definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "/usr/local/bin/web", arguments: [], scope: .user)
    d.at_boot @ true
    assert strings.contains(s: format_job(d).must(), substr: "<key>RunAtLoad</key>\n\t<true/>")

# A definition checked before anything is written: a program given by a
# relative path is refused, the system untouched.
test "install: a definition refused before anything is written"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")
    d = definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "web", arguments: [], scope: .system)

    match install(d)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

# The manager this system has.
test "manager: launchd on macOS"
    if os.system() != .darwin
        skip_test(reason: "this example is macOS's")
    assert manager().must() == .launchd

# Where a user's agent is written on macOS.
test "path_of: a user's agent on macOS"
    if os.system() != .darwin
        skip_test(reason: "this example is macOS's")
    assert strings.contains(
        s: path_of("org.example.web", scope: .user).must(),
        substr: "/Library/LaunchAgents/org.example.web.plist"
    )

# A service nobody installed.
test "status_of: a name nobody installed is not found"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")

    match status_of("org.example.surely-not-installed", scope: .system)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

# A stop asked of a service nobody installed.
test "stop: a name nobody installed is not found"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")

    match stop("org.example.surely-not-installed", scope: .system, limit: time.seconds(1))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

# A start asked of a service nobody installed.
test "start: a name nobody installed is not found"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")

    match start("org.example.surely-not-installed", scope: .system, limit: time.seconds(1))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

# A restart asked of a service nobody installed.
test "restart: a name nobody installed is not found"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")

    match restart("org.example.surely-not-installed", scope: .system, limit: time.seconds(1))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

# A removal asked of a service nobody installed.
test "remove: a name nobody installed is not found"
    if manager().is_err()
        skip_test(reason: "this system has no manager this package reaches")

    match remove("org.example.surely-not-installed", scope: .system, limit: time.seconds(1))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND || e.code == PERMISSION

# A definition with the plain choices.
test "definition: a system service that runs one program"
    d = definition(name: "org.example.web", program: "/usr/local/bin/web", arguments: ["--quiet"], scope: .system)
    assert d.arguments == ["--quiet"]
    assert d.restart == .never
